# Which skills get a directory in .claude/skills/ — and therefore a slot in every
# session's start-up roster.
#
# Everything NOT listed here is still fully present, as real content, in
# .claude/skill-library/. The `skill-library` skill indexes it by name,
# description and path, so an archived skill costs nothing until it is invoked
# and then hands over the exact file to read. Nothing is deleted and nothing is
# unreachable; the only thing this file decides is whether Claude pays for a
# skill's description at session start or on demand.
#
# Why prune at all: at 210 entries the roster overflowed and roughly 150 skills
# were listed by name with the description silently dropped. A skill whose
# description never reaches the model cannot be auto-invoked — it costs a line
# and buys nothing. There are 262 skills now, so the tier split is not optional.
# 70 entries is proven to render intact; this file holds 81, which is why the
# budget in bin/check-payload is 90 rather than "as many as fit".
#
# The bar — replacing the old "would this repo's TypeScript stack trigger it?",
# which was wrong for a system-wide setup:
#
#   1. the workflow rails — ID, Maestro, review and commit craft
#   2. the languages actually in use on this machine — Rust, Python, Nix, TS
#   3. trading and quant work
#   4. testing, debugging and design craft
#   5. anything a .claude/commands/*.md invokes by name
#
# Archiving is the default for anything new. A skill that should auto-invoke has
# to be argued for here.
#
#   bash .claude/harness/plugins/id-workflow/hooks/sync-skills.sh          apply
#   bash .claude/harness/plugins/id-workflow/hooks/sync-skills.sh --check  verify

